  <info><![CDATA[## WHAT IS IT?

The Cordon-Lite commuter model with every car driving on the real Auckland road network. Commuters live at their Cordon-Lite origins across Auckland and work in the city centre. Each one works in one building inside the cordon (SA3 "Auckland City Centre"), drawn at random once and kept for every day. On a replayed day a car leaves home, drives the TomTom major-road network through the morning traffic to the gate where its route enters the cordon, waits in its corridor's queue and drives on to its building.

## HOW IT WORKS

Road network. setup loads the TomTom major roads (`gis/tomtom_major_roads.shp`, converted from the prep GeoPackage by `python -m prep.build_netlogo_layers`) with the GIS extension and builds a node-link network the same way as the Python prep graph: a node at each segment end (NZTM rounded to 1 m), segments whose ends coincide dropped, and of parallel segments between the same two nodes the fastest kept (28,520 nodes, 30,846 roads). Only the giant component is kept, as the prep routes on it: 28,507 nodes and 30,835 roads, 1,106 nodes inside the cordon. The link weight is free-flow minutes (distance / speed limit). Nodes and links are hidden; the roads are drawn from the shapefile.

Routes. A commuter's gate is the prep gate of its origin (`../data/origins.csv`, `gate_id`; found through `origin_id` in the run's personas.csv, or the origin coordinates). Its car drives the free-flow shortest path from home to the outside end of the gate segment (a prefix of the prep path) and crosses the cordon at the gate point (`../data/gates.csv`). From the inside end it takes the shortest path to the cordon node nearest its building (in the largest connected set of cordon roads), with 60 minutes added to every road that has an end outside the cordon, so that a car that has passed its gate stays inside. On plain free-flow minutes about two thirds of these legs would leave the cordon and come back. A leg still leaves where the cordon's own roads do not connect: from the Wellesley Street ramp gate (about 100 to 160 m) and from Hopetoun Street (about 180 m). The car parks at that node (median 65 m from the building, up to about 780 m for buildings on the wharves, which the major-road network does not reach), and the commuter is drawn at the building on arrival. A folder whose agents are not prep origins (demo_scenario) queues each corridor at its corridor point instead; its homes are synthetic points, some kilometres from any road, so its cars start with a straight line and its speeds are not meaningful.

Road traffic. A run folder made with road congestion on (road_sections.csv, agent_routes.csv, road_profile.csv and road_params.csv) splits each commuter's route to its gate into road sections. Each minute a section carries background traffic (from the AT/NZTA traffic counts, rising through the morning) plus the commuters' own cars that entered it in the last 15 minutes, each agent standing for about 63 cars. The busier a section is for its capacity, the slower cars drive on it, so a car that leaves at 07:45 takes longer than one that leaves at 06:30, and fewer commuter cars ease the roads. This is the engine's procedure road-arrivals, the same as PyEngine, so every road delay equals the run's. Without these files (an older run folder, demo_scenario) the roads are free-flow and the road delay is 0. The roads inside the cordon are never congested: the gate queue stands for that.

Clock. The minutes are those of the Cordon-Lite engine. Gate arrival = departure + free-flow minutes to the gate (agents.csv) + road delay (whole minutes). The queue is the engine's Vickrey point queue per corridor (procedure point-queues, the same FIFO order, carry and cap as PyEngine), so every gate-exit minute equals the run's. Arrival = gate exit + free-flow minutes from the gate. Before its gate a car follows its section times from road-arrivals: it covers each section's free-flow minutes of route in the minutes the traffic gives it, so it slows down on busy sections, and it reaches the gate point exactly at its gate arrival minute (its times are stretched by about a minute at most for that, as the engine counts whole minutes). Without road files, and after the gate, a car moves along its route at constant speed. Cars are drawn every 15 simulated seconds (one tick). A day lasts until the last commuter has arrived, then the next day can start. The workplace building therefore sets where a car parks, not when it arrives: the engine's gate-to-destination minutes refer to the single prep destination, not to the building. In a 300-agent R-clock run with road congestion the implied speeds to the gate (route length / (gate arrival - departure)) are a median of 51 km/h on days 1 and 11 (5-95%: 22-81 km/h); at free flow they would be a median of 76 km/h (5-95%: 50-94 km/h). From the gate to the parking node the median is 40 km/h (5-95%: 13-72 km/h; a few short engine legs give up to about 95 km/h). Queued cars are red and are drawn back along the approach road from the gate. Each agent stands for about 63 cars (agents_represented / agents), so each takes up about 220 m of road (7 m per car, 2 lanes): the queue on the map is as long as the real queue.

Road colours. The road sections on the commuters' routes are drawn a little wider than other roads and coloured by how fast their traffic moves at the last 5-minute mark, as a share of the free-flow speed (the engine's speed for a car entering the section at that minute): 80% or more keeps the grey of the road, 60-80% (slow) is light yellow, 40-60% (heavy) orange and below 40% (jammed) purple. The slower the traffic, the wider the line. Red stays for queued cars and green for public transport, and the three road colours differ from the colours of the corridors' cars. The segments of each section come from `../data/section_segments.csv` (python -m prep.build_roads). Every 5 minutes only the sections whose colour changed are redrawn, at most 60 per tick, so when many roads change at once (07:30, 08:15, 09:15) the map catches up over a few simulated minutes; switching the view redraws the whole map at once.

PT riders leave home at their PT departure minute and appear at their building at their PT arrival minute (outcomes.csv of the run; without it they are at work all day). WFH and SKIP commuters stay at home.

Workplaces. One building per commuter, drawn uniformly from the 1,484 building points inside the cordon (`gis/cbd_buildings.csv`, one point inside each footprint) with a fixed seed, in agent_id order, so a replay always shows the same workplaces.

## HOW TO USE IT

1. Type a run folder in scenario-dir (default `../runs/R-clock_mock_py_s1`) and press setup (about 40 s).
2. Press go. One morning plays until everyone has arrived at work, and go stops. One tick is 15 simulated seconds. "traffic delay (min)" is the mean delay on the roads of today's cars that have reached their gate so far; "mean queue wait (min)" is today's mean wait at the gates.
3. To run day after day, switch keep-going? on before pressing go.
4. skip to end of day jumps to the end of the morning without animation. Auckland view and City-centre view switch the map; the City-centre view shows the gates and their queues best. The NetLogo speed slider sets the pace.

## THINGS TO NOTICE

There are two kinds of congestion: traffic on the roads into the city centre and the queue at each cordon gate. From about 07:00 the first roads turn yellow, orange or purple, mostly the commuters' own approaches; at 07:30 nearly all of the commuters' roads are yellow (slow), from about 08:15 most are orange (heavy) and the busiest approaches purple (jammed), and by about 09:15 more than half are grey again. Cars leaving in those hours slow down on the way and the "traffic delay (min)" monitor climbs. Watch the red queues grow behind the gates around 08:00 and the "waiting" counts in the City-centre view, before and after the charge starts; on charged days fewer cars drive in the peak, fewer roads turn purple and the traffic delay is lower. The plots use hours on the x-axis (7.5 = 07:30). On charged days the entries plot shows the charge, scaled so that its peak equals the highest bar.

## PYTHON ENGINE

This is also the engine Python drives headless (NetLogoEngine, `python -m cordonlite.run --engine netlogo`): it calls setup-from-dir, set-clock and run-day, which load no map and write outcomes_dayNN.csv, profile_dayNN.csv and road_dayNN.csv into the run folder. Watching a run in the GUI writes nothing.

## CREDITS AND REFERENCES

Vickrey, W.S. (1969) Congestion theory and transport investment. American Economic Review 59(2), 251-260.
Part of Cordon-Lite (Hyesop Shin and Milad Malekzadeh, CUPUM 2027). Road network: TomTom major roads. Buildings: LINZ building outlines inside the SA3 Auckland City Centre.]]></info>
